\title{Finite Moment Methods and Mean-Field Thresholds\\in Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi Random Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 3, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained treatment of finite first- and second-moment methods for the independent-edge random graph and connect them to two characteristic threshold scales. For an arbitrary finite family of prescribed edge sets, we derive the exact first moment of the number of realized patterns and the exact second moment as a sum indexed by pairwise unions. This yields an overlap expansion for the variance and a Paley--Zygmund-type lower bound for the probability that at least one pattern appears. We also prove a variance criterion under which absence has probability tending to zero. At the sparse scaling $p=\lambda/n$, we analyse the Poisson exploration fixed point $\rho=1-e^{-\lambda\rho}$: the only nonnegative solution is zero for $0<\lambda\leq1$, whereas a solution in $(0,1)$ exists for $\lambda>1$, with the quantitative lower bound $\rho\geq2(\lambda-1)/\lambda^2$. We explain how this identifies the mean-field giant-component transition and distinguish it from the later connectivity window $p=(\log n+c)/n$. Algorithms, applications, and the additional ingredients required for sharp finite-graph laws are discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi model $G(n,p)$ begins with $n$ labelled vertices and includes each of the $\binom n2$ possible undirected edges independently with probability $p$. Although individual edges are independent, graph structures are not: triangles can share edges, candidate routes can share links, and global connectivity is controlled by rare local obstructions near its threshold.

Threshold phenomena arise when a small change in $p$ produces a large change in the probability of a property. Two scales are especially important. At $p=\lambda/n$, expected degree approaches $\lambda$, and a macroscopic component first becomes possible when $\lambda$ passes through one. Connectivity appears later, at order $(\log n)/n$, when isolated vertices disappear.

Our finite mechanism is broadly reusable: count candidate structures, calculate how pairs overlap, and convert moment estimates into probability bounds. We establish exact finite identities and the phase transition of the limiting exploration fixed point. The full finite-graph laws for connectivity and giant-component size require further asymptotic inputs; we identify those inputs explicitly.

\section{The finite independent-edge model}

Let $E$ be a finite set of potential edges. A configuration is a subset $g\subseteq E$. For $p\in[0,1]$, assign mass
\[
w_p(g)=p^{|g|}(1-p)^{|E|-|g|}.
\]
The binomial theorem gives
\[
\sum_{g\subseteq E}w_p(g)=\sum_{k=0}^{|E|}\binom{|E|}{k}p^k(1-p)^{|E|-k}=1.
\]
For an event $\mathcal A\subseteq2^E$ and a real random variable $X$ define
\[
\Pp_p(\mathcal A)=\sum_{g\in\mathcal A}w_p(g),\qquad
\E_p[X]=\sum_{g\subseteq E}w_p(g)X(g),
\]
and define $\E_p[X^2]$ analogously.

\begin{definition}[Required-edge event]
For $A\subseteq E$, let $\mathcal A_A=\{g\subseteq E:A\subseteq g\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Probability of a required edge set]
For every $A\subseteq E$,
\[
\Pp_p(\mathcal A_A)=p^{|A|}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The edges in $A$ contribute $p^{|A|}$. Summing over arbitrary choices on $E\setminus A$ contributes
\[
\sum_{R\subseteq E\setminus A}p^{|R|}(1-p)^{|E\setminus A|-|R|}=1
\]
by the binomial theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Joint appearance]
For $A,B\subseteq E$,
\[
\mathcal A_A\cap\mathcal A_B=\mathcal A_{A\cup B},
\qquad
\Pp_p(\mathcal A_A\cap\mathcal A_B)=p^{|A\cup B|}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A configuration contains both sets exactly when it contains their union; apply the preceding lemma.
\end{proof}

Shared edges are therefore counted once. This elementary identity carries all pairwise dependence relevant to second moments.

\section{Pattern counts and first moments}

Let $I$ be finite and associate to each $i\in I$ a required edge set $S_i\subseteq E$.

\begin{definition}[Pattern count]
Set
\[
X(g)=\sum_{i\in I}\ind_{\mathcal A_{S_i}}(g)
=\#\{i\in I:S_i\subseteq g\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Expected Count Formula]
For every finite family $(S_i)_{i\in I}$,
\[
\E_p[X]=\sum_{i\in I}p^{|S_i|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $X$ into indicators and apply linearity:
\[
\E_p[X]=\sum_i\E_p[\ind_{\mathcal A_{S_i}}]
=\sum_i\Pp_p(\mathcal A_{S_i})=\sum_i p^{|S_i|}.
\]
Candidate events need not be independent.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First-Moment Vanishing Bound]
For $p\in[0,1]$,
\[
\Pp_p(X>0)\leq\sum_{i\in I}p^{|S_i|}=\E_p[X].
\]
Consequently, if $\E[X_n]\to0$, then $\Pp(X_n>0)\to0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The event $X>0$ is $\bigcup_i\mathcal A_{S_i}$. Apply the union bound and the required-edge probability formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Triangles]
There are $\binom n3$ possible triangles and each requires three edges, so
\[
\E[X_\triangle]=\binom n3p^3.
\]
The first-moment bound proves absence with high probability whenever this expression tends to zero.
\end{example}

\section{Exact second moments and overlap}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Second-Moment Formula]
For the pattern count above,
\[
\E_p[X^2]=\sum_{i\in I}\sum_{j\in I}p^{|S_i\cup S_j|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand
\[
X^2=\sum_{i,j}\ind_{\mathcal A_{S_i}}\ind_{\mathcal A_{S_j}}.
\]
The product is the indicator of $\mathcal A_{S_i}\cap\mathcal A_{S_j}=\mathcal A_{S_i\cup S_j}$. Take expectations and evaluate each joint probability.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact Variance Expansion]
\[
\Var_p(X)=\sum_{i,j\in I}p^{|S_i\cup S_j|}
-\left(\sum_{i\in I}p^{|S_i|}\right)^2.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\Var_p(X)=\sum_{i,j\in I}
\left(p^{|S_i\cup S_j|}-p^{|S_i|+|S_j|}\right).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the first- and second-moment identities into $\Var(X)=\E[X^2]-\E[X]^2$ and expand the square.
\end{proof}

Pairs with disjoint edge sets contribute zero to the covariance form. Thus second-moment analysis is an overlap-classification problem. For triangles, identical pairs have a three-edge union, distinct pairs sharing an edge have a five-edge union, and all other distinct pairs have disjoint edge sets and a six-edge union. Sharing only a vertex creates no edge dependence.

Given explicit sets, one can represent each by a bit mask. Computing every $|S_i\cup S_j|$ then takes $O(|I|^2)$ bit operations and $O(|I|)$ auxiliary storage. Symmetry can reduce this by replacing individual pairs with overlap classes.

\section{From moments to appearance}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Support Cauchy--Schwarz Bound]
If $X\geq0$, then
\[
\E_p[X]^2\leq\Pp_p(X>0)\E_p[X^2].
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $A=\{X>0\}$. Nonnegativity implies $X=X\ind_A$. Cauchy--Schwarz gives
\[
\E_p[X]^2=\E_p[X\ind_A]^2
\leq\E_p[X^2]\E_p[\ind_A^2]
=\E_p[X^2]\Pp_p(A).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Second-Moment Appearance Bound]
If $X\geq0$ and $\E_p[X^2]>0$, then
\[
\Pp_p(X>0)\geq\frac{\E_p[X]^2}{\E_p[X^2]}.
\]
For the family $(S_i)$,
\[
\Pp_p(X>0)\geq
\frac{\left(\sum_i p^{|S_i|}\right)^2}
{\sum_{i,j}p^{|S_i\cup S_j|}}.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Divide the preceding inequality by the positive second moment and substitute the exact formulas.
\end{proof}

If $\E[X^2]/\E[X]^2\to1$, this lower bound approaches one.

\begin{theorem}[Variance Criterion]
Let $X_n\geq0$ have nonzero means. If $\E[X_n]\to\infty$ and, for a constant $C$,
\[
\Var(X_n)\leq C\E[X_n]
\]
for all $n$, then $\Pp(X_n=0)\to0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On $X_n=0$, the squared deviation from the mean equals $\E[X_n]^2$. Hence
\[
\Pp(X_n=0)\leq\frac{\Var(X_n)}{\E[X_n]^2}
\leq\frac{C}{\E[X_n]}\longrightarrow0.
\]
\end{proof}

\section{Mean-field giant-component transition}

At $p=\lambda/n$, expected degree approaches $\lambda$. Early component exploration resembles a Poisson Galton--Watson process of mean $\lambda$. If $q$ is extinction probability, conditioning on the Poisson offspring number gives $q=e^{\lambda(q-1)}$. Thus survival probability $\rho=1-q$ satisfies
\[
\rho=1-e^{-\lambda\rho}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Mean-field order parameter]
For $\lambda>0$, a nonnegative $\rho$ is a survival parameter if $\rho=1-e^{-\lambda\rho}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp Fixed-Point Phase Transition]
If $0<\lambda\leq1$, the only nonnegative survival parameter is $\rho=0$. If $\lambda>1$, there exists a survival parameter with $0<\rho<1$. In particular, the critical order parameter at $\lambda=1$ is zero.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $f_\lambda(x)=1-e^{-\lambda x}-x$. Then
\[
f_\lambda(0)=0,\quad f_\lambda'(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}-1,
\quad f_\lambda''(x)=-\lambda^2e^{-\lambda x}<0.
\]
If $\lambda\leq1$, then $f_\lambda'(0)\leq0$, and strict concavity forces $f_\lambda(x)<0$ for $x>0$. If $\lambda>1$, then $f_\lambda'(0)>0$, so $f_\lambda$ is initially positive, while $f_\lambda(1)=-e^{-\lambda}<0$. Continuity gives a root in $(0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative Supercritical Lower Bound]
If $\lambda>1$ and $0<\rho<1$ is a survival parameter, then
\[
\rho\geq\frac{2(\lambda-1)}{\lambda^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $x\geq0$, $e^{-x}\leq1-x+x^2/2$. Therefore
\[
\rho=1-e^{-\lambda\rho}
\geq\lambda\rho-\frac{\lambda^2\rho^2}{2}.
\]
Rearrange and divide by the positive number $\rho$.
\end{proof}

This proves the transition for the limiting exploration equation. The finite-graph conclusion that the largest component has size $\rho n+o(n)$ also needs a branching-process coupling, concentration, and uniqueness of the macroscopic component.

\section{Connectivity and isolated vertices}

Connectivity occurs at a denser scale. If $I_n$ counts isolated vertices, then
\[
\E[I_n]=n(1-p)^{n-1}.
\]
For $p_n=(\log n+c)/n$, this tends to $e^{-c}$. The sharp connectivity target is
\[
\Pp(G(n,p_n)\text{ is connected})\longrightarrow e^{-e^{-c}}.
\]
A proof separates into two parts. First, one proves $I_n$ converges to a Poisson variable of mean $e^{-c}$. Its fixed falling-factorial moments satisfy the exact expression
\[
\E[(I_n)_k]=(n)_k(1-p_n)^{k(n-k)+\binom k2},
\]
which should converge to $e^{-ck}$. Second, one proves that the probability of being disconnected without isolated vertices tends to zero by summing over separated vertex sets of sizes between two and $n/2$. These asymptotic propositions go beyond the finite first- and second-moment identities alone.

\section{Algorithms, applications, and interpretation}

To sample $G(n,p)$, scan all unordered vertex pairs and include each edge after an independent uniform draw. A disjoint-set union data structure then computes component sizes in near-linear time in the realized edges, while pair scanning costs $O(n^2)$ draws.

For $\lambda>1$, the positive survival parameter can be computed from $f_\lambda(\rho)=0$ by bisection or by fixed-point iteration $\rho_{t+1}=1-e^{-\lambda\rho_t}$. Bisection reaches absolute error $\varepsilon$ in $O(\log(1/\varepsilon))$ iterations.

For explicit pattern families, compute
\[
M_1=\sum_i p^{|S_i|},\qquad M_2=\sum_{i,j}p^{|S_i\cup S_j|},
\]
and report $M_1^2/M_2$ when $M_2>0$. This gives a rigorous lower bound on appearance probability. The same pipeline applies to motif detection, network reliability through prescribed routes, and independent-feature certificate systems. In epidemic interpretation, the fixed point describes Poisson reproduction survival, though detailed network epidemic models require additional assumptions.

Simulation can display the rapid finite-size changes near $\lambda=1$ and $(\log n)/n$, but it does not prove asymptotic laws. Exact moment identities and analytic estimates control configurations that finite sampling may miss.

\section{Future directions}

Natural continuations are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item prove the Poisson isolated-vertex limit in the window $p_n=(\log n+c)/n$ by convergence of all fixed falling-factorial moments;
\item prove that absence of isolated vertices is asymptotically equivalent to connectivity in that window;
\item establish that for fixed $\lambda>1$ the largest component divided by $n$ converges in probability to the positive fixed point $\rho$, while the second-largest divided by $n$ tends to zero;
\item for $0<\lambda<1$ and $A>1/(\lambda-1-\log\lambda)$, prove that every component has at most $A\log n$ vertices with probability tending to one;
\item for fixed $r\geq3$, classify pairs of $r$-vertex sets by overlap and use the exact second moment to prove appearance of $K_r$ when $p_n n^{2/(r-1)}\to\infty$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Independent edges generate dependent structures, and pairwise unions measure that dependence exactly. The mean of a finite pattern count is a sum of single-copy probabilities; its second moment is a sum of pair-union probabilities. Cauchy--Schwarz converts overlap control into an appearance bound, and variance control yields high-probability existence. At the global level, the Poisson exploration equation changes sharply at mean degree one, while full connectivity occurs later in a logarithmic window governed by isolated vertices. These results provide both a finite counting toolkit and a precise map of the additional asymptotic work needed for complete random-graph threshold laws.

